🔥 1. `count`

👉 `count` is used to create multiple copies of the same resource

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  count         = 2
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
}

👉 This creates:

aws_instance.demo[0]
aws_instance.demo[1]

✅ Use case:

Create 2 EC2 instances of same type

⚠️ Limitation:

Resources are indexed by number, so changes can be harder to manage if list order changes

🔥 2. `for_each`

👉 `for_each` is used to create resources from a map or set

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  for_each      = toset(["dev", "stage"])
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  tags = {
    Name = each.key
  }
}

👉 This creates resources for:

dev
stage

✅ Better than `count` when resource identity matters

🔥 `count` vs `for_each`

`count` -> use when resources are almost identical
`for_each` -> use when each resource has a unique name or configuration

🧠 Interview Answer:

👉
"I use `count` for simple repeated resources and `for_each` when I need stable keys and per-resource customization."

🔥 3. `depends_on`

👉 Terraform usually detects dependencies automatically

But sometimes you need to define dependency manually

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  depends_on = [aws_security_group.app_sg]
}

👉 This ensures security group is created first

✅ Use case:

When dependency is not obvious in configuration

⚠️ Do not overuse it
Use only when required

🔥 4. `lifecycle`

👉 `lifecycle` controls how Terraform handles resource changes

Common options:

`create_before_destroy`
`prevent_destroy`
`ignore_changes`

Example:

resource "aws_instance" "demo" {
  ami           = "ami-0f5ee92e2d63afc18"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"

  lifecycle {
    create_before_destroy = true
  }
}

✅ Useful when replacing a resource without downtime

Example with `prevent_destroy`:

resource "aws_db_instance" "prod" {
  allocated_storage = 20
  engine            = "mysql"

  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy = true
  }
}

👉 Helps protect critical resources

🔥 5. `dynamic` Blocks

👉 `dynamic` blocks help generate nested blocks programmatically

Example:

dynamic "ingress" {
  for_each = var.ingress_ports
  content {
    from_port   = ingress.value
    to_port     = ingress.value
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

👉 Very useful in security groups and complex modules

✅ Use case:

Create multiple ingress rules without repeating code

🔥 6. Sensitive Variables

👉 Sensitive variables hide secret values in Terraform output

Example:

variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

👉 Good for:

Passwords
API keys
Tokens

⚠️ Important:
Sensitive variables reduce exposure in output, but secrets still need secure storage

Best practice:

Use secret managers or CI/CD secret storage

